# =============================================================================
# PRODUCTION environment — www.recycletechnologies.com
#
# Copy to  ~/apps/rti-website/.env.local  on the production server and fill it
# in. .env.local is git-ignored. Never commit the filled-in copy, and never
# reuse the dev server's values: every secret below is new for production.
#
#   chmod 600 .env.local
#
# Next reads this at BUILD time as well as at run time, so change it and then
# rebuild (npm run build), not just restart.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Indexing. The two lines that decide whether Google can see the site.
#
# DEPLOY_ENV=production turns on scripts/check-static.mjs's guard: the build
# FAILS if NEXT_PUBLIC_NOINDEX=true slips into a production build, which is the
# one mistake that would quietly de-index 600 ranking URLs.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_NOINDEX must be ABSENT (or false) here. With it off:
#   /robots.txt      Allow: /  + Sitemap line   (src/app/robots.ts)
#   <meta robots>    index, follow              (src/lib/seo.ts)
# The dev server keeps NEXT_PUBLIC_NOINDEX=true and DEPLOY_ENV=staging.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEPLOY_ENV=production
# NEXT_PUBLIC_NOINDEX=     <- leave unset in production

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Database — PostgreSQL 15+ on the same server, restored from the laptop
# dump (see deploy/PRODUCTION.md step 3). Its own user, its own password.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL=postgres://rti:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/rti_admin
# DB_POOL_MAX=10

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Admin sign-in. A NEW secret — not the laptop's, not dev's. Changing it
# signs everyone out. Generate on the server:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Leave ADMIN_ALLOW_HTTP unset: production is HTTPS only, so the session
# cookie is Secure.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Media library — the imported WordPress images and every admin upload
# (about 265 MB today). Outside the app folder so a redeploy can never delete
# it, and on the server's backup schedule.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEDIA_DIR=/var/lib/rti-website/uploads
# MEDIA_BASE_URL=/uploads

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Lead and newsletter email — Mailtrap SENDING (not the Sandbox dev uses;
# the Sandbox delivers to nobody).
#
# ALREADY SET UP (checked 23 Sep 2026): recycletechnologies.com is a verified
# sending domain in the shared Mailtrap account (aaqib@99technologies.com,
# Email API/SMTP), and the WordPress site sends through it today. SPF, DKIM
# (rwmt1/rwmt2), DMARC and the mt-link tracking record are all in DNS at
# Network Solutions. No DNS change for mail.
#
# SMTP_USER is literally "api". SMTP_PASS is the API token named
# rti-website-production (Admin on recycletechnologies.com only), which is
# separate from WordPress's token so either can be revoked alone. Paste it here
# on the server; it never goes in git or chat.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMTP_HOST=live.smtp.mailtrap.io
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=api
SMTP_PASS=
MAIL_FROM=RTI Website <noreply@recycletechnologies.com>
# What customers see as the sender of the automatic drop off email that
# residential enquiries get (src/data/emails.ts). Falls back to MAIL_FROM.
CUSTOMER_MAIL_FROM=Recycle Technologies <noreply@recycletechnologies.com>
# Who gets every enquiry, comma separated. The same two inboxes WordPress
# notifies today (Mailtrap Email Logs, 23 Sep 2026: contact and quote forms to
# usman@99technologies.com, the pickup form also to usman@ccdmarketing.com),
# so nothing changes for the people answering at launch. Also the Reply-To
# of the residential auto-reply, so a customer's reply reaches them.
LEAD_NOTIFY_TO=usman@99technologies.com,usman@ccdmarketing.com

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Build machine. One build worker peaks around 740 MB; leave blank on a
# server with memory to spare, or set it so workers x 740 MB fits in `free -h`.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUILD_CPUS=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Port the app listens on. nginx proxies 443 to it (deploy/nginx/).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=3000
